    <body>
      <sec id="article-36151.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The facial colliculus is an elevation of the 4th ventricular floor, formed by the abducens nucleus (cranial nerve VI) and the looping internal genu of the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). During embryogenesis, the abducens nucleus develops between 20 and 43 weeks of gestation, while facial nerve fibers form by week 7, looping dorsomedially over the abducens nucleus. The structure receives blood from the paramedian branches of the basilar artery and labyrinthine arteries.&#x000a0;Abducens and facial nerve fibers control horizontal eye movements and facial expression, respectively. Muscles influenced by this structure include the lateral rectus, orbicularis oculi, zygomaticus, buccinator, orbicularis oris, platysma, stapedius, digastric, and stylohyoid.</p>
        <p>The distinctive interaction between both cranial nerves can result in multilevel neurological dysfunction when lesions are present at the facial colliculus (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Pons Facial Colliculus Cross Section). Lesions can produce lateral gaze palsy, one-and-a-half syndrome, or Bell palsy, reflecting combined abducens and facial nerve involvement. Surgical approaches to the 4th ventricle, such as telovelar or transvermian, require precise identification of the facial colliculus to preserve these cranial nerves. Knowledge of the&#x000a0;anatomy and function of the facial colliculus allows clinicians to localize brainstem pathology accurately, anticipate neurological deficits, and guide safe surgical interventions while minimizing complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36151.s2" sec-type="Structure and Function">
        <title>Structure and Function</title>
        <p>The facial colliculus is located within the floor of the 4th ventricle, also known as the rhomboid fossa, lateral to the sulcus limitans, between the median fissure and superior fovea.&#x000a0;The structure is formed by the abducens nucleus, situated in the dorsal aspect of the caudal pons near the midline, and the internal genu of the facial nerve, positioned deeper within the tegmentum of the caudal pons.</p>
        <p>The internal genu of the facial nerve loops posteromedially around the ipsilateral abducens nucleus, producing a slight elevation of the 4th ventricular floor that is visible on axial magnetic resonance imaging. Although the facial colliculus does not perform an independent function, it serves as an essential anatomical landmark for identifying cranial nerves traversing this region (see <bold>Image</bold>. Brainstem). Lesions affecting this structure can disrupt the function of the embedded neurological components. (Sources: <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/book/monograph/9780323396325/fundamental-neuroscience-for-basic-and-clinical-applications?via=ihub%3D">Haines and Mihailoff</ext-link>, 2018; <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/book/monograph/9780323485630/skull-base-imaging?via=ihub%3D">Chong</ext-link>, 2018)</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36151.s3" sec-type="Embryology">
        <title>Embryology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Abducens Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <p>In the developing pons, the abducens nucleus becomes identifiable as a group of cells at approximately 20 weeks of gestation. The developing nucleus contains motor neurons and interneurons and undergoes marked growth between 20 and 43 weeks of gestation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Facial Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <p>During the 4th week of gestation, the chorda tympani arises rostrally from the facial nerve and travels ventrally toward the 1st pharyngeal pouch. By the end of week 5, the facial motor nucleus, derived from the 2nd pharyngeal arch, becomes identifiable within the metencephalon, the embryologic precursor of the pons.</p>
        <p>Between weeks 5 and 6, the development of the geniculate ganglion and the intermediate nerve begins. The intermediate nerve becomes recognizable as a distinct structure at approximately 7 weeks of gestation. Facial nerve fibers project dorsomedially toward the abducens nucleus and ascend approximately 2 mm before looping over the nucleus.</p>
        <p>Facial nerve fibers then course between the facial nucleus and the spinal trigeminal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V). Myelination of the abducens and facial motor roots begins as early as 17 weeks of gestation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36151.s4" sec-type="Blood Supply and Lymphatics">
        <title>Blood Supply and Lymphatics</title>
        <p>The paramedian branches of the basilar artery supply the abducens nucleus. Occlusion of these branches damages the abducens nerve and, consequently, the lateral rectus muscle innervated by the nerve, resulting in impaired coordination of horizontal eye movements. Patients may develop an inability to abduct the ipsilateral eye and adduct the contralateral eye. Further discussion of impaired eye movements appears below.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>The labyrinthine arteries, arising from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, supply the facial nucleus and associated nerve fibers. Occlusion of these arteries supplying the facial nerve may result in unilateral facial paralysis, impaired taste, loss of parasympathetic function, or diminished somatic sensation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36151.s5" sec-type="Nerves">
        <title>Nerves</title>
        <p>
<bold>Abducens Nucleus and Nerves</bold>
</p>
        <p>The abducens nucleus comprises motor neurons and interneurons. Motor axons arising from the abducens nucleus travel through the tegmental and basal regions of the pons and exit the brainstem at the pontomedullary junction. The axons then proceed rostrally and pass through the cavernous sinus adjacent to the sella turcica, which houses the pituitary gland. From the cavernous sinus, abducens nerve fibers pass through the superior orbital fissure and innervate the lateral rectus muscle of the ipsilateral eye. Contraction of the lateral rectus muscle produces outward movement of the eye toward the ear, or abduction (see <bold>Image</bold>. Brain and Spinal Cord).</p>
        <p>Interneurons originating in the abducens nucleus cross the midline and project rostrally through the contralateral medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), where synapses occur in the oculomotor nucleus&#x000a0;(cranial nerve III) in the midbrain. Oculomotor nerve fibers subsequently innervate the medial rectus muscle, producing inward movement of the eye toward the nose, or adduction. Functional integration of the abducens and oculomotor nerves coordinates horizontal eye movements.</p>
        <p>For example, the right abducens nerve innervates the ipsilateral lateral rectus muscle to abduct the right eye. Concurrently, an interneuron projects through the contralateral MLF to activate the medial rectus muscle of the left eye. Synchronized contraction of the ipsilateral lateral rectus and contralateral medial rectus enables conjugate gaze to the right. Lesions affecting different segments of this pathway produce distinct eye movement disorders, including lateral gaze palsy and one-and-a-half syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Facial Nucleus and Nerves&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The facial nerve comprises 2 components: the motor root and the intermediate nerve, also known as the nervus intermedius. The motor root carries somatic motor fibers to the muscles of facial expression, the stapedius, the posterior belly of the digastric, and the stylohyoid. The intermediate nerve, not part of the facial colliculus, carries taste, parasympathetic, and somatic sensory fibers. Motor and intermediate axons exit the brainstem and enter the internal acoustic meatus together with the vestibulocochlear nerve (cranial nerve VIII). Both components of the facial nerve then undergo a sharp turn within the facial canal, forming a bend known as the geniculate ganglion in the cranial cavity.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Intermediate Nerve&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intermediate nerve fibers do not loop around the abducens nucleus but, instead, pass through the internal acoustic meatus and join the facial motor nerve at the geniculate ganglion. The intermediate nerve contains parasympathetic and sensory fibers.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Parasympathetic fibers</bold>
</p>
        <p>The superior salivatory nucleus in the pons gives rise to preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that project to the submandibular and pterygopalatine ganglia. After leaving the superior salivatory nucleus, a subset of parasympathetic fibers reaches the geniculate ganglion and continues anteriorly as the greater petrosal nerve. The greater petrosal nerve joins sympathetic fibers of the deep petrosal nerve, forming the nerve of the pterygoid canal, also known as the vidian nerve.</p>
        <p>Within the pterygopalatine fossa, parasympathetic fibers of the vidian nerve synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion. Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers then project from the ganglion to innervate the lacrimal gland and mucous glands of the nasal and oral cavities.</p>
        <p>The remaining preganglionic parasympathetic fibers originating from the superior salivatory nucleus travel within the chorda tympani nerve. The chorda tympani joins the lingual nerve of the trigeminal nerve and continues to the submandibular ganglion, where synapses occur. Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers subsequently innervate the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands. The lingual nerve accompanies the chorda tympani but does not synapse in the submandibular ganglion. General somatic sensation from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue is carried by the lingual nerve.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Sensory fibers</bold>
</p>
        <p>The rostral nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla receives taste sensory fibers from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue. Taste from this region&#x000a0;is carried by the chorda tympani, which also conveys preganglionic parasympathetic fibers. Auricular branches of the facial nerve innervate the external ear and tympanic membrane and transmit general somatic sensation to the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve in the medulla. General somatic afferent fibers from the external ear and tympanic membrane pass through the stylomastoid foramen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Motor Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <p>Motor fibers of the facial nerve form the facial colliculus by projecting from the facial nucleus and looping around the abducens nucleus. After formation of the geniculate ganglion in the cranial cavity, the nerve to the stapedius arises from the motor component of the facial nerve. The nerve to the stapedius courses through the middle ear to innervate the stapedius muscle.</p>
        <p>Remaining motor branches exit the skull through the stylomastoid foramen. Branches to the posterior belly of the digastric, as well as the stylohyoid, arise before the facial nerve enters the parotid gland. Temporal, zygomatic, buccal, mandibular, and cervical branches innervate the 5 major groups of muscles responsible for facial expression. Contraction of these muscles enables a wide range of facial expressions. Damage to the facial nerve distal to the stylomastoid foramen produces a lower motor neuron lesion affecting the ipsilateral side of the face.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36151.s6" sec-type="Muscles">
        <title>Muscles</title>
        <p>
<bold>Lateral and Medial Rectus Muscles&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The lateral and medial rectus muscles are associated with motor neurons and interneurons of the abducens nerve, respectively. The lateral rectus muscle produces ipsilateral abduction of the eye, whereas the medial rectus muscle produces contralateral adduction of the eye. Coordinated contraction of these muscles enables horizontal eye movements.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Muscles of Facial Expression&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Extratemporal branches of the facial nerve innervate the unpaired orbicularis oris muscle and 23 paired muscles of facial expression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;The major muscles supplied by each facial nerve branch are as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Frontalis: The temporal branch, the uppermost branch of the extratemporal facial nerve, supplies the frontalis muscle on the forehead. The primary function of the frontalis muscle is to raise the eyebrows and wrinkle the forehead.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orbicularis oculi: The upper portion of the orbicularis oculi muscle is innervated by the temporal branch, whereas the lower portion receives innervation from the zygomatic branch of the facial nerve. The orbicularis oculi muscle around each eye helps close the eyelid and drain tears into the nasolacrimal duct.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Zygomaticus major: The zygomatic branch of the facial nerve innervates the zygomaticus major muscle. Contraction of the zygomaticus major elevates the angle of the mouth and contributes to smiling.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Buccinator:&#x000a0;The buccal branch of the facial nerve innervates the buccinator muscle. Contraction of this muscle&#x000a0;presses the cheek against the teeth during mastication and assists the orbicularis oris muscle in puckering the lips.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orbicularis oris:&#x000a0;The buccal branch of the facial nerve innervates the superior portion of the orbicularis oris muscle, whereas the marginal mandibular branch innervates the inferior portion. Contraction of the orbicularis oris muscle closes the mouth and lips.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Platysma:&#x000a0;The cervical branch of the facial nerve innervates the platysma muscle. The platysma extends from the neck to the mandible and functions to depress the corners of the mouth and assist in opening the mouth. The platysma is commonly referred to as the &#x0201c;fright&#x0201d; muscle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Coordinated activation of these muscles enables facial expression, speech articulation, and oral competence. Injury to extratemporal branches of the facial nerve disrupts these functions and produces characteristic patterns of facial weakness.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Stapedius</bold>
</p>
        <p>The stapedius muscle, attached to the neck of the stapes bone, is the smallest skeletal muscle in the body. Innervation is provided by the nerve to the stapedius, a branch of the facial nerve. Contraction of the stapedius reduces ossicular vibrations, protecting the inner ear from damage caused by loud sounds. Injury to the stapedius muscle or its nerve results in hyperacusis, an increased sensitivity to normal auditory stimuli.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Posterior Belly of the Digastric</bold>
</p>
        <p>The posterior belly of the digastric muscle attaches to the hyoid bone and the mastoid process of the temporal bone. Innervation is supplied by the digastric branch of the facial nerve. Coordinated contraction with the anterior belly of the digastric lowers the jaw to open the mouth and elevates the hyoid bone to facilitate swallowing.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Stylohyoid</bold>
</p>
        <p>The stylohyoid muscle extends from the styloid process of the temporal bone to the hyoid bone and receives motor innervation from the styloid branch of the facial nerve. Contraction of the stylohyoid elevates the tongue and retracts the hyoid bone.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36151.s7" sec-type="Surgical Considerations">
        <title>Surgical Considerations</title>
        <p>The telovelar and transvermian approaches are commonly employed techniques for accessing pathologies within the 4th ventricle, allowing optimal exposure of the caudal 2/3. The facial colliculus serves as a critical landmark in these procedures, and accurate identification is essential for preserving the abducens and facial nerves in this region. Tumor-related distortion may obscure visualization of the facial colliculus striae, increasing the risk of nerve injury during both approaches. Neurophysiological mapping can facilitate the precise identification of the facial colliculus during surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36151.s8" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>The facial colliculus contains the abducens and facial nerves.&#x000a0;Lesions affecting this structure can produce multilevel neurological deficits. Injury to the abducens nerve may impair horizontal eye movements, manifesting as lateral gaze palsy or one-and-a-half syndrome. Damage to the facial nerve can result in Bell palsy.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Lateral Gaze Palsy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Lesions of the abducens nucleus cause paralysis of the ipsilateral lateral rectus muscle. Although the contralateral medial rectus remains functional via oculomotor nerve innervation, interneurons projecting from the damaged abducens nucleus&#x000a0;cannot transmit signals through the contralateral MLF to the oculomotor nucleus. Consequently, horizontal eye movements cannot be coordinated when both eyes attempt to gaze toward the side of the lesion.</p>
        <p>For example, lesions of the right abducens nucleus impair the right lateral rectus muscle by disrupting motor fibers projecting from the nucleus. The oculomotor nucleus and its fibers remain intact, preserving the function of the left medial rectus when independent of abducens input. Damage to the right abducens nucleus also disrupts interneurons crossing through the left MLF to the left oculomotor nucleus, preventing activation of the left medial rectus. Consequently, horizontal gaze toward the right is impaired, with both eyes fixed straight ahead. Gaze toward the left remains normal.</p>
        <p>
<bold>One-and-a-Half Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>One-and-a-half syndrome is defined by impaired horizontal eye movement. Abducens nerve fibers originate from the ipsilateral abducens nucleus and project to the ipsilateral lateral rectus muscle. Interneurons arising from the same nucleus cross the midline, ascend through the contralateral MLF, and synapse on the contralateral oculomotor nucleus, which innervates the contralateral medial rectus. The ipsilateral MLF carries fibers from the contralateral abducens nucleus to the ipsilateral oculomotor nucleus, which innervates the ipsilateral medial rectus muscle. Extensive lesions of the dorsal pontine tegmentum, exceeding the size of the abducens nucleus, can damage both the ipsilateral abducens nucleus and MLF, producing deficits in the ipsilateral lateral rectus and bilateral medial rectus muscles and severely impairing horizontal gaze.</p>
        <p>For instance, lesions involving the right abducens nucleus and MLF impair the right lateral rectus and left medial rectus muscles associated with right abducens fibers. The right medial rectus, innervated by the right oculomotor nerve, cannot receive input from the damaged right MLF and is also impaired. Horizontal gaze toward the right is lost, and both eyes remain directed straight ahead. Horizontal gaze toward the left is limited to movement of the left eye, while the right eye remains fixed in a straight-ahead position.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Bell Palsy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Looping of the facial motor nerve fibers over the abducens nucleus in the dorsal caudal pons renders the facial colliculus vulnerable to injury.&#x000a0;Pathology&#x000a0;involving the facial colliculus produces paralysis of the ipsilateral facial muscles, characteristic of Bell palsy. Clinical features include drooping of the mouth corner and upper eyelid, inability to wrinkle the forehead, and generalized weakness of facial expression muscles. Since facial motor and intermediate fibers converge only at the geniculate ganglion, taste, parasympathetic, and somatic sensory functions may remain intact.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36151.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyperacusis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Injury to the facial nerve can paralyze the stapedius muscle, permitting excessive stapes movement. This abnormal motion results in increased sensitivity to normal sound levels, known as hyperacusis.</p>
      </sec>
